course: dictionary
lesson: 15055
title: Words about giving things back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are built from parts you already know. Returnable is made of return (to give or send back) and -able (can be done). So returnable means able to be given back. «These bottles are returnable.» You can take them back to the shop and get some money.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If a sign says «Returnable bottles: 10p each», the bottles can go back for money. Ask yourself what would fit there. Then try "can be given back" in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: The word has a second meaning, often found in formal writing. «The form is returnable by Friday.» Here it means the form must be given back by a certain day. The word «by» is the clue: it tells you there is a deadline.
explain: Watch for the small word non-. «Non-returnable» means it cannot be sent back. So a non-returnable item cannot be given back, which is the opposite of returnable.
fact: Returnable means able to be given or sent back.
fact: «Returnable» can also mean must be given back by a certain time, as in «The form is returnable by Friday.»
fact: In some countries, such as Canada, Australia and Ireland, returnable bottles and cans earn a deposit: a small amount of money you get back when you return them.
fact: «Non-returnable» means it cannot be given back.
fact: Schools often send letters home saying «Permission slips returnable by Friday.»
exercise: «These glass bottles are returnable, so you can get money back.» What does returnable mean? | Very heavy | Dirty and broken | *Able to be taken back for money | Good for a picnic
exercise: «The form is returnable by Friday.» What does returnable mean here? | *It must be given back by Friday | It is made of paper | It is a Friday holiday | It has a picture of Friday on it
exercise: Ella reads «non-returnable» on a shop sign. What does it tell her? | It is returnable with a receipt, always | It is free to take home | *It cannot be given back | It can be given back for money
test: «The library books are returnable by Monday.» What does the word tell you? | They cannot be borrowed at all | It is a day that starts with M | They are for sale on Monday | *They must be given back by Monday
test: Zara sees «Returnable cans: 10c each» in Ireland. What does this mean? | *You get some money back when you bring the can back | The cans cost 10c and cannot be used | The cans are very old | They are made only for rain
exam: Which sentence uses returnable in the formal way, meaning must be given back by a certain time? | Your bottles are returnable for money | The shop sells returnable bags | *The forms are returnable by Thursday | Returnable is a kind of fish
